<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pharaoh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as pharaonic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pharaoh is the modern title for the kings of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, regarded as divine rulers responsible for maintaining cosmic order. The institution reached the height of its power in the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Foreign rulers, including the Kushite kings, the Persian kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>, adopted pharaonic titles and imagery to legitimise their rule over Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pharaoh.jpg" alt="Pharaoh" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Double_crown.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This tolerance for local institutions was a deliberate strategy: Cyrus framed the conquest of Babylon as the restoration of Marduk's favour, and the Achaemenid kings consistently sought legitimacy within the religious frameworks of their subjects, ruling as <b>pharaoh</b> in Egypt and as restorer of religious order in Babylonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Hammurabi and his successors, became the classical standard of the language and the basis of the Babylonian that was the diplomatic lingua franca of the Near East for centuries afterwards: Egyptian <b>pharaohs</b>, Hittite kings and the rulers of smaller states corresponded in it, as the Amarna archive demonstrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/babylonian-empire">Babylonian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first half of the third millennium BCE a fishing and farming village grew into a walled town with stone houses and temples, and by about 2600 BCE it was a city with close ties to the <b>pharaohs</b> of the Old Kingdom of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…was found in the palace, as were fragments of Egyptian stone vessels inscribed with the names of the <b>pharaohs</b> Khafre and Pepi I. The tablets were written in the Sumerian cuneiform script, used both for Sumerian words and for the local Semitic language, Eblaite, which is usually classed…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Ahmose I, founding <b>pharaoh</b> of the Eighteenth Dynasty, completed this expulsion around 1550 BCE, reunited the country, reconquered Nubian territories lost during the preceding fragmentation, and began rebuilding the administrative and military institutions that would sustain the empire for nearly five centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike the Egyptian <b>pharaoh</b>, the Hittite king was not a living god but the earthly steward of the storm god Tarhun, and became divine only after death.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
